        "evolution": "Originally maritime in nature, 'equip' entered English via Middle French in the 16th century to describe fitting out warships with gear and provisions. 'Lease' entered legal English via Anglo-Norman property contracts conveying real estate for specified terms. During the post-WWII corporate boom and the introduction of commercial photocopiers (notably the Xerox 914 in 1959), companies shifted toward operating leases to conserve liquid capital and hedge against rapid technological obsolescence. 'Equipment lease' became a cornerstone of commercial corporate finance and administrative procurement.",
