        "exampleQuestion": "Before signing the new equipment ______ for the multifunction printers, the procurement committee insisted on adding a clause guaranteeing 24-hour on-site technical support. (A) lease (B) leased (C) leasing (D) leaser",
        "tips": "In TOEIC Part 5, test-makers frequently test compound noun structures involving corporate procurement and contractual terminology. In 'the new equipment ______', the noun adjunct 'equipment' modifies the missing head noun. Here, 'lease' (A) is the standard countable noun signifying the contractual agreement itself. Choice (B) 'leased' is a past participle/adjective, (C) 'leasing' is a gerund/uncountable process noun (which does not fit naturally after the singular article 'the new ... for'), and (D) 'leaser' is an uncommon obsolete form for 'lessor'. In Part 7, reading passages frequently feature correspondence negotiating the renewal, extension, or termination of an equipment lease."
