        "evolution": "'Remind' was formed in 17th-century English from the prefix 're-' (again) and 'mind' (from Old English 'gemynd', meaning memory or intellect), meaning 'to bring back into the conscious mind'. The agent noun 'reminder' emerged around the 1660s. 'Note' entered Middle English from Old French 'note' and Latin 'nota' (a mark, cipher, sign, or short writing). Combining the two into 'reminder note' became common in 19th-century commercial correspondence and clerical practice to describe brief written slips or cards sent to prompt action on an overdue bill, impending appointment, or pending correspondence before formal legal notices were dispatched.",
